{"task":"Bind generated sites to loopback in both modes","goals":"Close the LAN exposure on the HTTPS sites, which was the concern left open on the previous commit","status":"Completed","recorded_at":"2026-09-09T08:44:18Z","body":"Closes the concern left open on ac41876: the HTTPS sites were reachable from the LAN, with a certificate warning as the only obstacle and file_server behind it. Measured before the change from this machine's own LAN address: matsu.ldev:443, matsu-dev.ldev:8444 and seasonal-drops.ldev:8443 all returned 200. After it, all refuse.\n\nThe macOS rule this ran into is worth keeping. An unprivileged process may bind the WILDCARD address on a port below 1024, but not a specific one: `*:443` succeeds where `127.0.0.1:443` is refused. So a plain `bind` is only available above 1024 unless the process is root — and Caddy applies a site's bind to the HTTP->HTTPS redirect server it generates on :80, which is a port the file never mentions. Caddy aborts the whole config on one bad listener, so the symptom is every site in the file going down over a permission error about :80. Hence `auto_https disable_redirects` alongside the bind. Nothing is lost by it: each site block already redirects plain HTTP on its own port.\n\nAuto mode is unaffected by the limit because it runs as a root LaunchDaemon, so its :443 and :80 blocks bind loopback directly.\n\nThe live per-site Caddyfiles under ~/Sites are not in this repo and were updated separately. matsu.ldev moved off 443 onto 8444 in the process, so the bare https://matsu.ldev/ URL is now https://matsu.ldev:8444/.\n","parent_commit":"ac418767ad3340cf3a79b6c992a276ef51f1c8f8","repo":"ldev","pct":"100","tasks":[{"id":"persite-bind","content":"Bind each generated per-site block to 127.0.0.1 and ::1","status":"completed"},{"id":"persite-redirects","content":"Emit auto_https disable_redirects, because the generated :80 redirect server inherits the bind and cannot hold a privileged port as a user","status":"completed"},{"id":"persite-one-global","content":"Fold the admin port into the single leading global block and say why a second keyless block is fatal","status":"completed"},{"id":"auto-bind","content":"Bind auto mode's *.<TLD>, bare <TLD> and ask-endpoint blocks to loopback — safe there because auto mode runs as a root LaunchDaemon","status":"completed"},{"id":"record-1024","content":"Record the macOS rule that cost the time: an unprivileged process can bind the wildcard on a port below 1024 but not a specific address, so *:443 works where 127.0.0.1:443 is refused","status":"completed"},{"id":"verify-live","content":"Verified end to end: a site rendered from the template starts as this user, listens only on 127.0.0.1/::1, answers 200 locally and refuses on 192.168.1.152. Both live ldev sites do the same, and ldev doctor passes","status":"completed"}],"agent":"claude-code","changelog":[{"text":"`templates/Caddyfile.auto.tmpl`: added `bind 127.0.0.1 ::1` to the wildcard `*.__TLD__` block and to the bare-TLD dashboard block, restricting both HTTPS listeners to loopback.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"complete","explicit":false,"tags":["security"]},{"text":"`templates/Caddyfile.auto.tmpl`: added `bind 127.0.0.1` to the `http://127.0.0.1:__ASK_PORT__` ask endpoint so the address matches its Host-header spelling, plus a comment explaining that the address token alone was only a match, not a bind.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"complete","explicit":false,"tags":["security","docs"]},{"text":"`templates/Caddyfile.persite.tmpl`: added `auto_https disable_redirects` to the global block, because Caddy's generated :80 redirect server inherits the site `bind` and an unprivileged process cannot bind `127.0.0.1:80`.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"complete","explicit":false,"tags":["build-config"]},{"text":"`templates/Caddyfile.persite.tmpl`: added `bind 127.0.0.1 ::1` to the site block, with comments on how to opt back into LAN serving and on the macOS privileged-port constraint.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"complete","explicit":false,"tags":["security","docs"]},{"text":"Comment-only: documented the one-keyless-block-must-come-first Caddy rule in the per-site global block.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"complete","explicit":false,"tags":["docs"]}],"code_quality":[{"text":"OK.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"complete","explicit":false}],"vulnerabilities":[{"text":"No new vulnerabilities; the change closes one. Previously every `*.__TLD__` site and the dashboard were bound on all interfaces, with the on-demand TLS certificate as the only obstacle — `curl -k` from any LAN host reached `file_server browse` site roots. Loopback binding is the correct fix.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"complete","explicit":false,"tags":["security"]},{"text":"Auto mode's `*.__TLD__` and bare-TLD blocks bind loopback on privileged :443 while per-site mode needed `auto_https disable_redirects` for exactly this reason. That asymmetry is only safe if auto mode's Caddy runs as root (or with `CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE`); if it can ever run unprivileged, the same `127.0.0.1:80`/`:443` permission-denied failure takes down the whole config. Worth confirming against the install path.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"needs_review","tags":["security","compatibility"]}],"code_style_suggestions":[{"text":"OK.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"complete","explicit":false}],"reusability":[{"text":"OK — the two templates duplicate the `bind 127.0.0.1 ::1` line and its rationale, which is unavoidable in Caddyfile templates.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"complete","explicit":false}],"system_compatibility":[{"text":"`bind ... ::1` assumes IPv6 loopback is present; on a host with IPv6 fully disabled Caddy will fail the listener and abort the whole config. Rare on macOS defaults, but it is the failure mode to expect.","kind":"info","priority":"low","state":"needs_review","tags":["compatibility"]}],"review_r